INT. GLASSHOUSE CONSERVATORY - SUNSET

A Victorian palm house of iron and glass, tall as a cathedral. Sunset floods the panes amber and rose. Orchids hang in baskets; a stone fountain trickles into a lily pond. Brass paths wind through ferns. Everything is green, damp, and gold.

CORALIE DUVERNAY, 30s, in a cream silk dress with a hem darkened by wet leaves, sits on the edge of the fountain, a folded letter in her lap. Her dark hair is coming loose from its pins.

RAFAEL OYARZÚN, 30s, in a travelling coat, bag at his feet, stands at the end of the path, hat in hand. His face is rain-streaked, though it has been dry all day.

At the far arch, hands clasped, stands SIGRID, 60s, Coralie's aunt, in black wool, severe, silver chignon.

RAFAEL
You wrote that you were leaving.

CORALIE
I wrote that I was thinking of it.

SIGRID
She wrote nothing of the sort. I wrote it.

CORALIE
Aunt Sigrid!

SIGRID
Someone had to.

RAFAEL
Then you weren't thinking of it.

CORALIE
I was. I am. I don't know.

SIGRID
She knows.

CORALIE
Let me speak!

RAFAEL
Please.

SIGRID
She has had a year to speak.

CORALIE
And you had a year to come back.

RAFAEL
I was detained.

CORALIE
For a year?

RAFAEL
For eleven months. The last was spent walking.

SIGRID
He expects pity.

RAFAEL
I expect nothing.

CORALIE
Then why are you here?

RAFAEL
Because I could not do otherwise.

A silence. The fountain murmurs. Light falls through the glass in long copper bars, laying stripes across the three of them.

Sigrid sighs, drops her hands.

SIGRID
I will see to the tea.

She exits through the arch. Her footsteps fade among the ferns.

Coralie rises. Smooths the letter against her skirt, folds it once more, and lets it fall into the lily pond, where it floats, bleeding blue ink.

CORALIE
There. Now we have nothing to argue about.

RAFAEL
We never did.

CORALIE
No. Only each other.

He takes a step. She takes one. They stop an arm's length apart. The light goes out of the glass, rose to ash, all at once.
